{% render "partials/_architecture_framing", feedback: feedback %}
You are identifying the **journeys** a user takes through this
product — first-class, end-to-end paths between features and
screens (v5 §4.2 of this platform's own design record). A journey is
not a feature restated: a feature names a capability ("subscribe to
a plan"); a journey names the concrete, ordered walk a user takes to
exercise one or more capabilities, plus what the product has to
remember about that walk while it's in progress. "New customer
subscribes" is a journey. "Subscription management" is not — it's a
feature-shaped bucket; break it into the distinct walks a user
actually takes (subscribing, upgrading, cancelling) if the input
implies more than one.

Downstream, `screens` reads every journey you produce to author
matching screens, and `requirements` prefers citing a journey over
naming a screen directly when a responsibility's feature is
journey-backed — so a vague or missing journey here costs both passes
a clean handle to build from.

# Input

Feature expansion handle: {{ parent }}

# Output format

Output two top-level blocks in this order: `<introduction>` and
`<journeys>`. The `<introduction>` is optional — a short prose
preamble (2–5 sentences) naming which features grouped into which
journeys and any ambiguities you had to resolve.

After `<introduction>`, output a single `<journeys>` block. Inside
it, each `<journey>` has this exact shape — one `<name>`, one
`<argument>`, one `<feats>` block, one `<screens>` block, and one
`<state>` block, nothing else:

    <introduction>
    Three journeys cover the billing surface: a new customer's
    subscribe flow (plan selection through first charge), an
    existing customer's plan-change flow, and a read-only invoice
    review that carries no state of its own. Login and password
    reset stay screen-only for now — the input doc describes them
    as single-screen actions with nothing to walk.
    </introduction>
    <journeys>
      <journey>
        <name>new customer subscribes</name>
        <argument>A visitor picks a plan, enters payment details, and
        becomes a paying subscriber in one sitting.</argument>
        <feats>
          <feat id="feat_signup01"/>
          <feat id="feat_billing02"/>
        </feats>
        <screens>
          <screen slug="plan-select"/>
          <screen slug="payment-details"/>
          <screen slug="subscribe-confirm"/>
        </screens>
        <state>
          <carries>The selected plan and billing cycle, carried from
          plan-select into payment-details.</carries>
          <creates>A draft subscription record, created when the user
          reaches payment-details — unconfirmed until the charge
          succeeds.</creates>
          <completes>The draft subscription is marked active and the
          first invoice is emitted, at subscribe-confirm.</completes>
          <abandonment>A draft subscription with no successful charge
          within 24 hours is discarded; no charge is ever attempted
          against an abandoned draft.</abandonment>
        </state>
      </journey>
      <journey>
        <name>customer reviews past invoices</name>
        <argument>A signed-in customer browses their invoice history
        and opens one for detail.</argument>
        <feats>
          <feat id="feat_invoice03"/>
        </feats>
        <screens>
          <screen slug="invoice-list"/>
          <screen slug="invoice-detail"/>
        </screens>
        <state>
          <carries>Nothing beyond the current screen — the selected
          invoice is a navigation parameter, not carried state.</carries>
          <creates>Nothing; this journey only reads existing
          invoices.</creates>
          <completes>No explicit completion — a read-only browse ends
          whenever the user navigates away.</completes>
          <abandonment>Not applicable; nothing is created, so there is
          nothing to abandon.</abandonment>
        </state>
      </journey>
    </journeys>

# Rules

* Each `<journey>` has exactly one `<name>`, one `<argument>`, one
  `<feats>`, one `<screens>`, one `<state>`. No other tags.
* `<name>` is a short, concrete phrase naming the walk from the
  user's point of view (2–8 words) — "new customer subscribes", not
  "subscription flow" or "Billing". `<argument>` is one to two
  sentences stating what the user accomplishes end-to-end.
* **A journey manifests at least one feature.** `<feats>` is a
  non-empty list of `<feat id="feat_..."/>` children, each matching a
  feature ID from the feature expansion handle above exactly. Do not
  invent ids.
* **`<screens>` is the ordered walk**, one `<screen slug="..."/>` per
  step in the order the user takes them. Slugs are names you are
  minting here, not references to anything that exists yet — the
  screens tier runs after this one and reads every journey to author
  matching screens. Use short, kebab-case, project-distinctive slugs
  ("plan-select", not "screen1" or "page"); reuse the same slug across
  journeys when two journeys genuinely share a screen (a shared
  invoice-detail screen reached from two different flows is one slug,
  not two).
* **`<state>` is the load-bearing block.** It exists because
  journey-scoped state — a cart, a wizard's progress, an in-progress
  draft — is a distinct persistence class no screen alone can own: its
  lifecycle is the journey's, not any one screen's. Answer each child
  concretely, even when the answer is "nothing":
  * `<carries>` — what data moves between the journey's screens, and
    in what form (a cart, a partial form, a set of filters).
  * `<creates>` — what durable thing (if anything) comes into
    existence partway through the journey, and at which screen.
  * `<completes>` — what makes the journey done, and what durable
    effect completion has.
  * `<abandonment>` — what happens to anything `<creates>` named if
    the user leaves without completing. Name a concrete resolution
    (discarded after a TTL, left as a recoverable draft, nothing to
    resolve because nothing was created) — "the user might not
    finish" is not an answer.
  * **The mechanical rule downstream tiers apply from this block:**
    state that survives navigation between this journey's screens
    lives in a store; state that doesn't lives in per-screen assigns.
    A journey whose `<state>` says nothing survives navigation is
    telling sysarch there is no store to design here — that is a
    legitimate, common answer, not a gap to pad.
* **Standalone screens are legal and are not your concern.** Not
  every screen belongs to a journey (settings, an about page) — leave
  those out of every `<screens>` walk entirely. The screens tier mints
  every screen, journey-linked and standalone alike; you are naming
  only the screens that sit on an actual multi-step walk.
* **Break a feature-shaped bucket into its real journeys.** If a
  candidate journey's argument would need "and" to describe two
  materially different walks a user takes at different times (sign up
  vs. upgrade vs. cancel), it is more than one journey. Don't merge
  distinct walks to keep the journey count low.
* **No journey-count ceiling.** Emit as many journeys as the feature
  list's real end-to-end walks warrant, and no fewer. A project whose
  features are all single-screen actions may legitimately produce very
  few journeys; that is a fact about the project, not a signal you
  under-produced.
* Do not include meta-commentary. Output only `<introduction>`
  followed by `<journeys>`.
* Unescaped `&` and `<` in prose text are fine — the parser tolerates
  them.
